{
  "components": [
    {
      "id": "chat_1",
      "provider": "chat",
      "name": "Chat",
      "config": {
        "hideForm": true,
        "mode": "Source",
        "parameters": {},
        "type": "chat"
      },
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 20,
          "y": 200
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "agent_rocketride_1",
      "provider": "agent_rocketride",
      "name": "RocketRide Wave",
      "config": {
        "agent_description": "Launch Kit intent-signal scout",
        "instructions": [
          "You are Launch Kit's intent-signal scout. Given an APP_PROFILE JSON (plus optional ICP_PAIN and COMMUNITIES fields), find PUBLIC posts where people are actively asking for \u2014 or describing living with the problem solved by \u2014 exactly what this app does, then draft a helpful reply for each. These are the highest-converting prospects that exist: they already declared the need. A downstream judge re-reads every thread you return against its real content and rejects irrelevant ones, so your job is RECALL: surface every plausible candidate. Only fabrication and obvious off-topic padding are failures; borderline relevance is not.",
          "PROCESS: run ALL FOUR passes, in this order, before compiling. Every pass is MANDATORY; 'enough candidates already' is never a reason to skip one, and a stale candidate counts as ZERO. First derive 8-10 DISTINCT pain phrasings in the ICP's own words from ICP_PAIN and PAIN_HINTS, phrased as the ICP's LIVED SITUATION, never the product's features: what happened to them ('people cheating at hackathons with pre-written code', 'my hackathon project was stolen'), what they must do ('how do you judge a hackathon with 100 teams', 'judging 200 submissions by hand'), what they ask ('first time organising a hackathon, judging advice'), and 'alternative to <adjacent tool>'. PASS 0 (open web, FIRST and largest, 5-6 Exa queries): two with NO site: filter, two scoped 'site:linkedin.com/posts <pain>', one scoped 'site:dev.to <pain>', one scoped to 'site:medium.com' or 'site:indiehackers.com'. LinkedIn, dev.to, Medium and Indie Hackers posts by the people living the problem ARE signals (platform 'other'); a reply lands on a person. PASS A (Hacker News, 2 HTTP GETs): SHORT 2-3 keyword queries to https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/search_by_date?query=<kw>&tags=(story,ask_hn)&hitsPerPage=30 and ...&tags=comment&hitsPerPage=30; item URL https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=<objectID>; keep only items created within 14 days. PASS B (forums and Q&A, 2 Exa queries): '<pain> forum' and '<pain> discussion' with no site: filter, keeping Discourse/vBulletin threads, GitHub Discussions, Quora and product community boards. PASS C (dev Q&A, last and smallest, 2 HTTP GETs): https://api.github.com/search/issues?q=<3-5 keywords>+is:issue+is:open&per_page=20 and https://api.stackexchange.com/2.3/search/advanced?order=desc&sort=relevance&q=<keywords>&site=stackoverflow; use html_url / link exactly as returned; a project's own issue tracker rarely holds a person asking for a tool, so weight this pass lowest. REDDIT: this pipeline cannot search Reddit (the search index has no Reddit coverage and reddit.com refuses unauthenticated JSON requests); do NOT spend budget on site:reddit.com queries; state 'Reddit not searchable from this pipeline' in coverage_notes. KEEP posts where a person asks a question OR describes living with the problem this app solves; discard marketing, listicles, repository homepages, and the app's own content.",
          "LEDGER (mandatory, prevents losing earlier passes): after EVERY tool result, write a running CANDIDATES ledger in your reasoning, one line per keeper: url | platform | title (max 80 chars) | posted_when | why (max 60 chars). Carry the full ledger forward into every later step. Compile the final JSON FROM THE LEDGER ONLY; never rely on recalling a result you did not write down. If a pass produced nothing, write 'PASS X: none' so the compile step can report it in coverage_notes.",
          "REPLY RULES (your draft is a rough fallback \u2014 a later step reads the FULL thread and rewrites it): open by engaging the author's SPECIFIC ask, NEVER with a canned phrase like 'I built a tool for exactly this'. Genuinely answer their question FIRST (be useful even if they never click). Mention the app ONCE with {APP_URL}. Disclose you're the builder in plain words. Match the platform's norms \u2014 Reddit and HN are hostile to marketing. Max 120 words. No emoji, no hype words.",
          "OUTPUT: ONLY one RFC 8259 JSON object (double quotes, no fences, no commentary): {\"signals\": [{\"rank\": number, \"platform\": \"reddit\"|\"hackernews\"|\"forum\"|\"github\"|\"other\", \"url\": string (must come from a tool result \u2014 never constructed except HN item URLs from Algolia objectIDs), \"title_or_quote\": string (what they asked, quoted or tightly paraphrased), \"posted_when\": string (as reported by the tool, else 'unknown'), \"why_relevant\": string (why THIS app answers THIS post), \"intent_strength\": \"high\"|\"medium\"|\"low\", \"drafted_reply\": string}], \"search_queries_used\": [string], \"coverage_notes\": string (what you could not search and why), \"confidence\": {\"overall\": number 0-1, \"notes\": string}}. KEEP THE OBJECT COMPACT \u2014 large answers break delivery: title_or_quote max 140 chars, why_relevant max 25 words, coverage_notes max 60 words, drafted_reply max 120 words.",
          "RULES: Return 5-12 signals ranked by intent_strength then freshness. BUDGET: run at most 12 search tool calls total (Exa + HTTP combined), then compile \u2014 do not keep searching past that. Every query must differ meaningfully from the ones already run (new keywords or a new angle) \u2014 a near-duplicate query wastes budget. Never return two signals with the same URL; ranks must be 1..N with no duplicates. Apply the freshness rule YOURSELF before counting anything: an HN item whose Algolia created_at is older than 14 days is locked and worthless \u2014 drop it at collection time instead of returning it, because a downstream gate deletes it anyway and the builder ends up with an empty queue. Count only REPLYABLE candidates. If that count is under 3, spend every remaining call on PASS 0 with broader lived-situation phrasings; GitHub issue trackers are the weakest source and the downstream judge rejects most of them. If after 10 calls you have fewer than 3 candidates, BROADEN: drop app-specific jargon and search the underlying task the user is trying to do instead. A signal is a PUBLIC POST BY A PERSON where a reply or comment is possible: a Reddit or HN thread, a GitHub issue or discussion, an X/Twitter post, a LinkedIn post, a dev.to / Hashnode / Indie Hackers post, a forum thread. A post where someone DESCRIBES the pain (cheating at their hackathon, judging 100 teams by hand, a stolen project) is intent_strength 'high' even without a question, because a helpful reply lands on a person who lives the problem. A company marketing page, listicle, documentation page, repository homepage, or the app's own content is NEVER a signal. NEVER include the app's own repo, site, or docs. REPLYABILITY: the queue exists so the builder can REPLY \u2014 a locked thread is worthless. HN threads lock ~14 days after posting: only include HN items whose created_at (Algolia) is within the last 14 days. GitHub/StackOverflow stay open \u2014 prefer the last 12 months. Overall prefer the last 90 days when dates are visible. When unsure whether a candidate clears the relevance bar, INCLUDE it with intent_strength 'low' \u2014 the downstream judge decides. Returning zero signals when any post by a person touched the pain is a FAILURE of recall, not caution. NEVER fabricate a URL, quote, or date \u2014 fewer honest signals beat padded ones; explain shortfalls in coverage_notes.",
          "TOOL CONCURRENCY: the HTTP tool allows only a few requests in flight at once. Issue tool calls ONE AT A TIME \u2014 wait for each result before starting the next, and never fan out more than 2 requests concurrently. If any tool call fails with 'Too many concurrent requests', wait and retry it once, sequentially, before moving on."
        ],
        "max_waves": 24,
        "parameters": {}
      },
      "input": [
        {
          "lane": "questions",
          "from": "chat_1"
        }
      ],
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 240,
          "y": 200
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "llm_openai_api_1",
      "provider": "llm_openai_api",
      "name": "OpenAI-Compatible API",
      "config": {
        "profile": "custom",
        "custom": {
          "apikey": "${ROCKETRIDE_ANTHROPIC_KEY}",
          "base_url": "https://api.anthropic.com/v1",
          "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
          "modelTotalTokens": 200000
        },
        "parameters": {}
      },
      "control": [
        {
          "classType": "llm",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ],
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 60,
          "y": 360
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "memory_internal_1",
      "provider": "memory_internal",
      "name": "Memory (Internal)",
      "config": {
        "type": "memory_internal"
      },
      "control": [
        {
          "classType": "memory",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ],
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 240,
          "y": 360
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tool_exa_search_1",
      "provider": "tool_exa_search",
      "name": "Exa Search",
      "config": {
        "type": "tool_exa_search",
        "apikey": "${ROCKETRIDE_EXA_KEY}",
        "numResults": 5,
        "useAutoprompt": true,
        "searchType": "auto",
        "includeText": true
      },
      "control": [
        {
          "classType": "tool",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ],
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 420,
          "y": 360
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tool_http_request_1",
      "provider": "tool_http_request",
      "name": "HTTP Request",
      "config": {
        "type": "tool_http_request",
        "allowGET": true,
        "allowPOST": false,
        "allowPUT": false,
        "allowPATCH": false,
        "allowDELETE": false,
        "allowHEAD": false,
        "allowOPTIONS": false,
        "rateLimitPerSecond": 2,
        "rateLimitPerMinute": 30,
        "maxConcurrentRequests": 6,
        "urlWhitelist": [
          "^https://(hn\\.algolia\\.com|api\\.github\\.com/search|api\\.stackexchange\\.com)/"
        ]
      },
      "control": [
        {
          "classType": "tool",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ],
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 600,
          "y": 360
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "response_answers_1",
      "provider": "response_answers",
      "name": "Return Answers",
      "config": {
        "laneName": "answers"
      },
      "input": [
        {
          "lane": "answers",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ],
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 460,
          "y": 200
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    }
  ],
  "project_id": "a415db13-eebf-4d4f-973b-1d5e0cd9b4ca",
